rom \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\).\n\nIf two distinct points \\(P_i,P_j\\) (\\(i\\neq j\\)) lie on the same line, that line must have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-j)- (n+1-i)}{j-i}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus any line that contains two of the points \\(P_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is **not** sunny.\n\nConsequently, to cover the \\(n\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_n\\) we need at least  \n\n* one line of slope \\(-1\\) (which can contain any number of the \\(P_i\\)), and  \n* for each remaining point a distinct line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\).\n\nSince a line of slope \\(-1\\) is not sunny, at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny:\nthe line of slope \\(-1\\) and the line that covers the first point not lying on it.\nTherefore the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound: construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-2;\n  \\]\n\n* the two diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence they are not sunny.\nThey are pairwise distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\)