nly.  Consequently the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nmust also belong to the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\).  This line is non\u2011sunny as well.\n\nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound: construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(S_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical lines } \\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-2\n&&\\text{(there are }n-k-2\\text{ of them);} \\\\\n&\\ell_{1}:\\;x+y=n,\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.\nThey are pairwise distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nQ_t=\\bigl(n-k-1+t,\\;t+1\\bigr)\\in S_n,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_t:\\; y-(t+1)=t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is \\(t\\); since \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\nBecause the slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k-2\\).*  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is one of the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nThe point lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\), respectively.\n\n*Case 3: \\(a\\ge n-k-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nt=a-(n-k-1)\\quad(\\;0\\le t\\le k\\;).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t=0\\) then \\(a=n-k-1\\) and \\(b\\le k+1\\); such a point satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (the line \\(x+y=n\\)).  \n\nIf \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k-1+t)=k+2-t\\).  \nFrom the definition of \\(\\sigma_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\; y = t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr)+t+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=n-k-1+t\\) gives \\(y=t+1\\); increasing \\(x\\) by one raises \\(y\\) by \\(t\\).  \nHence the set of integer points of \\(\\sigma_t\\) with abscissa at least \\(n-k-1+t\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(n-k-1+t+s,\\;t+1+ts\\bigr)\\mid s\\ge0\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(s=0\\) we obtain \\(Q_t\\); for \\(s\\ge0\\) the second coordinate equals \\(t+1+ts\\ge t+1\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le k+2-t\\) and \\(t\\le k\\), we have \\(b\\le t+1+ t\\cdot 0\\); thus the only possible point of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) and satisfying the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely \\((a,b)=(a,t+1)\\), which lies on \\(\\sigma_t\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of the lines listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) as sunny lines; all the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Hence the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of \u00a72 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
