roved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** – show that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   must contain at least two non‑sunny lines.  \n   *The \\(n\\) points \\((i,n+1-i)\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) all lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\); this line is therefore non‑sunny.*  \n   *The \\(n\\) points with abscissa \\(1\\), namely \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\), are all on the vertical line \\(x=1\\). Any line that contains two of them is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), hence non‑sunny. Since we have only \\(n-1\\) lines left after using the \\(-1\\) line, we cannot cover all these \\(n\\) points with sunny lines; consequently the vertical line \\(x=1\\) must also be present.*  \n   Thus at least two lines are non‑sunny, so the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound** – for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we construct an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   *Non‑sunny part:*  \n   – vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-2\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\));  \n   – the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   *Sunny part:* for each \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n   \\[\n   Q_t=\\bigl(n-k-1+t,\\;t+1\\bigr),\\qquad \n   \\sigma_t:\\; y-(t+1)=t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); the \\(\\sigma_t\\) are pairwise distinct, hence sunny.  \n\n   One checks that every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of the above lines:\n   * if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le n-k-2\\) it lies on the corresponding vertical line;\n   * if \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on one of the two diagonal lines;\n   * otherwise \\(a\\ge n-k-1\\) and the point lies on the unique sunny line \\(\\sigma_t\\) with \\(t=a-(n-k-1)\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, proving that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nSince the upper and lower bounds coincide, the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume that \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*The line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nall satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\); hence they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) is **not** sunny, and it must be one of the \\(L_j\\).\n\n*The vertical line \\(x=1\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nV_i=(1,i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAny two of them have the same abscissa, so any line containing two of them is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), i.e. non‑sunny.  \nIf we tried to cover all the points \\(V_i\\) with sunny lines, each \\(V_i\\) would have to lie on a different line, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate cannot be joined by a sunny line.  Thus we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines for the set \\(\\{V_i\\}\\).\n\nBut after using \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) we have only \\(n-1\\) lines left.  Hence it is impossible to cover all the points \\(V_i\\) with sunny lines only.  Consequently the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nmust also belong to the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\).  This line is non‑sunny as well.\n\nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, and the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound: construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(S_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical lines } \\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-2\n&&\\text{(there are }n-k-2\\text{ of them);} \\\\\n&\\ell_{1}:\\;x+y=n,\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore they are non‑sunny.\nThey are pairwise distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nQ_t=\\bigl(n-k-1+t,\\;t+1\\bigr)\\in S_n,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_t:\\; y-(t+1)=t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is \\(t\\); since \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\nBecause the slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k-2\\).*  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is one of the non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a+b=n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nThe point lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) or \\(\\ell_{2}\\), respectively.\n\n*Case 3: \\(a\\ge n-k-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nt=a-(n-k-1)\\quad(\\;0\\le t\\le k\\;).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t=0\\) then \\(a=n-k-1\\) and \\(b\\le k+1\\); such a point satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (the line \\(x+y=n\\)).  \n\nIf \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k-1+t)=k+2-t\\).  \nFrom the definition of \\(\\sigma_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\; y = t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr)+t+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=n-k-1+t\\) gives \\(y=t+1\\); increasing \\(x\\) by one raises \\(y\\) by \\(t\\).  \nHence the set of integer points of \\(\\sigma_t\\) with abscissa at least \\(n-k-1+t\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(n-k-1+t+s,\\;t+1+ts\\bigr)\\mid s\\ge0\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(s=0\\) we obtain \\(Q_t\\); for \\(s\\ge0\\) the second coordinate equals \\(t+1+ts\\ge t+1\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le k+2-t\\) and \\(t\\le k\\), we have \\(b\\le t+1+ t\\cdot 0\\); thus the only possible point of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) and satisfying the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely \\((a,b)=(a,t+1)\\), which lies on \\(\\sigma_t\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of the lines listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) as sunny lines; all the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  Hence the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of §2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY*