a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(a=1\\), \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b\\in\\{n,n+1\\}\\); hence it lies on one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (b)  The case \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non‑sunny line)\n\nUse only the mandatory line \\(\\ell_{-1}:x+y=n+1\\) as the single non‑sunny line.\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define a sunny line\n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_i:\\; y = i\\,x - i^{2}+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\Sigma_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\Sigma_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct.\nMoreover \\(\\Sigma_i\\) passes through the point \\((i+1,i+1)\\in S_n\\) because\n\n\\[\ni(i+1)-i^{2}+1 = i+1 .\n\\]\n\nNow consider any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).*\n*Otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Set \\(i=a-1\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\)).  \nThen \\(b = a+b-a = (i+1)+b-a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(b\\le n-i-1\\), whence \\(b = i+1\\).  \nThus \\((a,b) = (i+1,i+1)\\) and belongs to \\(\\Sigma_i\\).*\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_{-1}\\)) is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (c)  Intermediate values for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(n-4\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWrite \\(m=n-1-k\\) (so \\(1\\le m\\le 3\\)).  \nWe will use \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines*  \nTake the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and, if \\(m\\ge2\\), also the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nIf \\(m=3\\) we add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nThus we have exactly \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Sunny lines*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_t:\\; y-(t+1)=t\\bigl(x-(n-k-1+t)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\Sigma_t\\) is \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); the slopes are distinct, so the \\(\\Sigma_t\\) are pairwise different sunny lines.\nEach \\(\\Sigma_t\\) passes through the point  \n