st \\(n\\) points.\nThe horizontal case is symmetric.  \nFor a line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer\nsolutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow suppose that among the \\(n\\) covering lines at most one is non‑sunny.\nLet the (possible) non‑sunny line contain at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 1.2)\nand each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines contain at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points (Lemma 1.1).  Hence the total\nnumber of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}=n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,(n-1).\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(n=2m\\) is even, then  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}=2m+m(2m-1)=2m^2-m<2m^2+m=\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nand if \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, then  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}= (2m+1)+(m+1)(2m)=2m^2+3m+1<2m^2+3m+2=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a covering by \\(n\\) lines that contains **at most one** non‑sunny line\ncannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently **at least two** of the\n\\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-2\\) lines are pairwise distinct and non‑sunny.  \nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence after drawing all \\(L_i\\) the uncovered points are exactly those whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinate is either \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  Explicitly,\n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigl\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{(n,1)\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(U\\) contains **three** points, independent of \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Adding the sunny lines  \n\nWe still have to add \\(k\\) more lines (so that the total number becomes\n\\(n\\)).  Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(k\\ge 0\\) and we may have\n\\(k\\le 3\\) or \\(k>3\\).  The construction works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \nChoose three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of the three\npoints of \\(U\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\).  This is possible:\npick a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of\nthe lines already used; because the set \\(S_n\\) is finite, we can avoid all\nother points by a suitable choice of the intercept.  The remaining\n\\(k-3\\) sunny lines are taken to be **empty** (they contain no point of\n\\(S_n\\)); for instance the lines  \n\n\\[\ny=2x+M,\\qquad M\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;M\\gg n\n\\]\n\nare sunny and lie completely outside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  All these\nlines are distinct, sunny, and together with the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines they\nform a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=2\\) or \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=0\\).*  \nProceed analogously: pick \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines through \\(k\\) distinct\npoints of \\(U\\) (possible because \\(|U|=3\\ge k\\)), and let the remaining\n\\(n-(n-2)-k=2-k\\) lines be the two vertical lines \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\) (both\nnon‑sunny).  When \\(k=0\\) we simply keep the two extra vertical lines and\ndo not add any sunny line.\n\nIn every case we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the other \\(n-k\\) lines are\nnon‑sunny, and all points of \\(S_n\\) lie on at least one of the lines.\n\n---------