its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound: at least three non‑sunny lines are needed  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (non‑sunny line)\nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope\n\\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nIn each case the integer solutions satisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are at most \\(n\\):\nfor \\(x=c\\) we need \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and then \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\le n\\); the\nother two cases are symmetric. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (sunny line)\nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nIf \\(m>0\\) then moving from one lattice point on the line to the next changes\n\\(x\\) by at least \\(1\\) and \\(y\\) by at least \\(1\\); consequently the sum\n\\(x+y\\) increases by at least \\(1+m\\ge2\\).  Since all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy\n\\(x+y\\le n+1\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points can lie on the line.\nFor \\(m<0\\) (and \\(m\\neq-1\\)) the same bound follows after reflecting the plane\nin the line \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines contains **exactly two** non‑sunny lines, say \\(L_1,L_2\\), and the remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines are sunny.\n\nRemove from \\(S_n\\) all points lying on \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each of \\(L_1,L_2\\) contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so at least\n\n\\[\n|S_n|-2n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(3,1),\\qquad P_2=(3,2),\\qquad P_3=(4,1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and none of th